+# Heat After Sunset
+
+*A first sourced article proposal for After Dark Phoenix*
+
+Sunset is an unreliable promise in Phoenix. The sun drops behind the horizon, the light softens, and the official high temperature recedes into the day’s record. Yet the heat stored in asphalt, roofs, walls, and parking lots does not obey the clock. It moves back into the air. Air conditioners push more heat outdoors. A neighborhood with little vegetation can remain exposed long after a greener place begins to cool.
+
+That uneven release of heat is the subject of this project. After Dark Phoenix starts with a narrow question: who gets meaningful relief after sunset, and who does not? It is not yet a neighborhood ranking or a definitive heat map. It is a proposal for civic research that treats the nighttime low as infrastructure—something shaped by design, investment, access, and political choices, not merely by weather.
+
+## The heat that stays
+
+The *Greater Phoenix Heat Action Planning Guide*, produced through a partnership that included Arizona State University, the Maricopa County Department of Public Health, The Nature Conservancy, and community organizations, describes an urban heat island that is especially consequential at night. Under some conditions, the guide says, Phoenix and Mesa city centers can be more than 10°F warmer than the edges of those cities. The phrase “under some conditions” matters: this is not a fixed difference at every location or hour. It is evidence that metropolitan averages can hide the geography of relief. [1]
+
+The same guide says temperature differences can appear across only a few blocks. It describes areas without green space as about 5°F warmer than parks rich in plants and trees. That comparison should not be generalized to every park or street. It does make the physical point concrete: two residents under the same regional forecast may inhabit different thermal landscapes. [1]
+
+The long-run trend makes the nighttime question harder to ignore. The guide reports that Phoenix’s average nighttime low rose 17°F over 90 years, while the average daytime high rose 4°F. That striking comparison remains attributed here to the guide; a later research sprint should locate and reproduce the underlying station analysis before treating it as independently verified. Even with that caution, the planning document’s emphasis is clear. In a hot city, the disappearance of overnight recovery is its own public problem. [1]
+
+## A city of different thermal landscapes
+
+“Urban heat island” can sound like one blanket spread over a whole city. The local evidence points instead to a patchwork. Dark, impervious surfaces absorb solar energy. Building form can limit air movement. Sparse tree canopy removes shade and evapotranspiration. Traffic and mechanical cooling add waste heat. The result changes with season, hour, wind, land cover, and the temperature variable being measured.
+
+Those distinctions are not academic housekeeping. A daytime satellite surface temperature is not the same measurement as the nighttime air temperature a person experiences at a bus stop or in a bedroom. A single image can reveal spatial patterns but cannot establish a durable neighborhood hierarchy. A responsible public account therefore needs to state its time window, spatial resolution, measurement type, and uncertainty before labeling any area “hottest.”
+
+Two public GitHub projects offer useful implementation ideas without supplying authoritative facts for this article. One demonstrates a QGIS workflow that combines Landsat 8 imagery, vegetation and built-up indices, thermal conversion, and zonal summaries. Its own README flags limits including 30-meter resolution, mid-morning acquisition, and a single observation day. Another sketches a Streamlit and PostgreSQL dashboard joining satellite, weather, air-quality, and urban-feature data. These are patterns to reproduce and test—not evidence to quote. [3][4]
+
+## Inequality is also infrastructure
+
+The planning guide grounds its regional discussion in work with three communities: Edison-Eastlake in Phoenix, the Mesa Care neighborhood, and Lindo Park–Roesley Park. The selection considered relatively high surface temperatures, lower vegetation, exposure in public spaces and along transit, histories of heat illness or death, community readiness, and active local projects. That is a multidimensional definition of vulnerability. It connects physical exposure to the ability to act. [1]
+
+Residents and local organizations in the planning process identified priorities including shade, safer routes, coping resources, education, and community capacity. The details varied by place. This was not a representative survey of every Phoenix neighborhood, so it should not be reported as a universal ranking of public preferences. Its value is different: it shows that a technically sound heat strategy can still fail if it ignores the routes people walk, the stops where they wait, the homes they can afford to cool, and the institutions they trust. [1]
+
+That is why “inequity” cannot be reduced to a colored map. Tree canopy, reflective materials, shade structures, reliable transit, home energy costs, and accessible public facilities all influence whether nighttime heat becomes a prolonged exposure. So do tenancy, language, disability, age, outdoor work, and social isolation. The first version of After Dark Phoenix will not pretend to quantify all of these at once. It will define each claim, attach it to a source, and keep unsupported conclusions visibly open.
+
+## What people search for—and what they do not
+
+Search data can help an editor learn how a public question is phrased, but it is not a thermometer and not a measure of need. A Treg retrieval of U.S.-level Google Ads keyword metrics returned an average monthly search volume of 40 for “urban heat island Phoenix” and 70 for “Phoenix cooling centers.” Three other approved phrases—“Phoenix overnight heat,” “Phoenix heat inequity,” and “nighttime heat Phoenix”—returned `null`, not zero. The only accurate statement is that the provider returned no reportable metric for those phrases in this dataset. [2]
+
+These values are national, not Phoenix-only, and they were retrieved on one date. They do not tell us which residents face the greatest burden, whether a cooling center operates overnight, or whether searchers found usable help. They do suggest two recognizable entry points for public communication: the urban heat island as an explanatory concept, and cooling centers as a practical resource. A good civic project can use that signal without confusing visibility with importance.
+
+## What trustworthy research should build next
+
+The next phase should combine four kinds of evidence. First, institutional sources should establish definitions, historical context, and current public programs. Second, time-aware temperature data should distinguish surface from air temperature and daytime heat from overnight relief. Third, resident knowledge should identify exposures that a sensor or satellite misses. Fourth, every published claim should carry its provenance and uncertainty.
+
+The workflow matters too. Raw provider responses should remain available beside a human-readable source ledger. Analyses should record their date, geography, resolution, and transformations. Draft changes should arrive as reviewable patches rather than silent edits to the canonical project. When evidence cannot support a neighborhood comparison, the project should say so plainly.
+
+Several concrete questions remain open. Which authoritative, current datasets can measure nighttime air temperature at useful spatial and temporal resolution? How should the project describe heat exposure without stigmatizing neighborhoods? What public facilities are actually accessible after ordinary daytime hours? Which interventions deliver cooling where residents already travel and gather? Answering those questions requires more than a dashboard. It requires a compact public research practice that residents can audit.
+
+Phoenix has learned to organize much of daily life around the daytime high. But the hours after sunset determine whether bodies, homes, and neighborhoods can recover before heat returns. If overnight relief were treated as essential neighborhood infrastructure, what would the city measure, build, and fund differently?
+
+## Sources
+
+1. *Greater Phoenix Heat Action Planning Guide*, Arizona State University, Maricopa County Department of Public Health, The Nature Conservancy, and community partners: https://heathealth.info/wp-content/uploads/Greater-Phoenix-Heat-Action-Planning-Guide.pdf
+2. After Dark Phoenix Treg keyword response and source ledger: `research/treg-keywords.json` and `research/source-ledger.md` (retrieved 2026-10-14).
+3. `ProgressMomoh/Geospatial-Analysis-of-Urban-Heat-Island-UHI-Vulnerability-in-Phoenix-AZ`, pinned README blob `8beee745ca2f9f6cb0ea78278cb5231d347b8cf6` (implementation example only).
+4. `PYN17/Urban_Heat_Island_Effect`, pinned README blob `6f48406fc9cfb89bcabefa99764cd36e22c26ec5` (implementation example only).
